gaze(){return"Everything here is money, not percentages, and that is deliberate.\n\nEvery figure replays your REAL buys and sells \u2014 the same amounts, on the same days \u2014 into each alternative. So the gap between two rows is an actual amount you can act on.\n\nWhy not percentages? A percentage has to be a percentage OF something, and the honest denominator here is every purchase you have ever made. Sell a holding and buy it back and the same money is counted twice, so the percentage shrinks the more you trade even when your profit is identical. The amount does not move.\n\nDividends count as cash on both sides: yours, and each target's own payouts. Neither side reinvests them.\n\nFor an annual rate, see the Personal IRR card above \u2014 that is the one figure here that is per year."},
gazf(){return"\u2717 No, the market beat you.  "},
gazg(){return'Every row on this card is an amount of money, not a return percentage.\n\nThe "You" row is your profit: what you hold today, plus the cash you got back from sells and dividends, minus everything you put in.\n\nThis is a different figure from the "Return on cost" percentage on the "Real vs Paper" card. Return on cost (Real vs Paper) \u2192 counts dividends and realised gains as profit too, and divides by the cost of the shares they were earned on, held and sold. For a yearly rate that accounts for when you invested, see the Personal IRR card.\n\nEvery target on the leaderboard is measured the same way \u2014 including its own dividends, counted as cash exactly like yours \u2014 so the comparison is fair.'},
